A||N||14||FieroJoe||Famous Window Scratches||||1||
Z||000000||FieroJoe||12-09-2000||10:25 PM||||I have all new dewstrips to replace my original ones on my 87 coupe, and the scratches are pretty bad on the driver's side.  The local auto glass place said that Jewler's Rouge w/water, and a felt pad at low speeds can sometimes take out scratches.<P>   Anybody have good luck with this?  I thought that it might be worth a try before getting a whole new piece of glass installed.||||reg||1
Z||000001||terryk||12-09-2000||11:07 PM||||I've never been able to polish out scratches I can feel with my finger nail. If I can't feel them, they are likely to polish out.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fiero_Freak||12-09-2000||11:37 PM||||Yeah... you can really heat up that window fast and break it if you're not careful.  Ummmm... not that I would know a think about that.  hehe  All I have to say is thank god for that tint.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Sndfr||12-10-2000||12:51 AM||||Ya i got those window scratches too.  So i can go to any jewerly store and they have that "rouge" stuff?<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/scorchio/sevil.gif">      <B>Matthew "Whyt Nyte" D.</B>      <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/scorchio/sgrin.gif">    <BR><B><I>84 SE</B>- automatic, rare Fender Delete option </I><BR>    <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/ut2/redcap.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/ut1/mgwhore.gif"> ||||reg||2
Z||000004||Oreif||12-10-2000||01:04 AM||||I used the "Rain-X" glass cleaner/polish. <BR>Like terryk said it will remove the small scratches but the deeper ones will still be there. I have also heard that toothpaste works well too, But I have never tried it.||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroJoe||12-10-2000||05:33 PM||||Jewler's Rouge is just a really fine buffing/polishing compound.  You can get it at hardware stores.<BR>    I was already planning on replacing the glass, I thought it might be worth a shot.<P><BR>Thanks all!||||reg||1
Z||000006||alpha||12-10-2000||07:27 PM||||You can use cerium oxide to polish out scatches. I know JC Whitless sells a kit. I am told there are better products as well. A good glass shop should be able to do it for you.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://fiero.dhs.org/fiero/sig.jpg">   <BR>   <A HREF="mailto:douglas_knight@umit.maine.edu">douglas_knight@umit.maine.edu</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||bubbajoexx||12-15-2000||09:04 PM||||You will find it easer to repace the glass than to use the rouge its alot of work to remove scratches and they do not always come out  good luck. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/bongo.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||12-15-2000||11:01 PM||||<A HREF="http://www.eastwoodco.com" TARGET=_blank>Eastwood</A> has a kit for this. I don't know much about it. they have a pretty decent rep. Haven't delt with them myself yet. I'm thinking of getting that powder coater system they have in the not distant future.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Eric_PA||12-17-2000||11:13 AM||||Beleive it or not you can use toothpaste to remove shallow scratches in glass. I have an uncle that is a jewler and he uses it to get rid of fine scratches in watch faces. I just noticed on my 84 that the drivers side window is scratched. I'm going to try it today.||||reg||1
Z||000010||BAM-BAM||12-17-2000||12:08 PM|||| It's alot easier to replace the glass at $55.00 each and you just sit back and watch it happen. Or you could spend hours and energy and still not have what you want.<BR>Just my 2.<P> Peace, B2 OUR!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000011||theogre||12-17-2000||01:37 PM||||On the side windows I have to agree with that. At least as long as the windows are available and fairly cheap. Also depends how bad the scratches are. A few fine ones will likely come out nice. If it's bad like one of mine is then replacement is the only way out. <P>Windshield glass however is expensive. It's at least worth trying it on. Worst thing that happens is you still need a new windsheild.||||reg||1
Z||000012||rogergarrison||12-17-2000||03:56 PM||||i can buy used glass at yard for $10-$15 so why mess with trying to polish them out. My glass installer will even put them in for $50||||reg||1
Z||000013||FieroJoe||12-17-2000||05:40 PM||||Yeah I can probably polish out the passenger ones, but the drivers side I can get my fingernail in, so they are deep.<BR>    Where can I get glass that cheap?!?!  The best estimate that I had was $135 installed.  Im sure if I can find the glass and have them just install it I would save some.<P>Any ideas where I can get an cheap piece of driver's side glass without any scratches at all?<BR><P>------------------<BR>FieroJoe<BR>c311t@yahoo.com<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||rogergarrison||12-18-2000||01:04 PM||||you just have to shop the yards til u find a good one. also note that glass shops have a huge markup, so deal with them. I get $300-$400 windshields installed all the time in my shop for $150 or less. My Fiero kitkar windshield cost me $115 installed and with new moldings.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556367"> ||||reg||1
